data.content:
Since there's no `.csproj` visible, I'll give you the two best lightweight options:

---

### Option 1 — .NET 8+ `TimeProvider` (recommended)

.NET 8 ships an abstract `TimeProvider` class. No new types needed:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use the built-in fake:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 14, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

(`FakeTimeProvider` is in `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`.)

### Option 2 — Pre-.NET 8: thin `IClock` interface

```csharp
public interface IClock
{
    DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; }
}

public class SystemClock : IClock
{
    public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
}
```

Same pattern — default to `SystemClock` in the constructor, pass a stub in tests.

---

**`TimeProvider`** is the lightest path if you're on .NET 8+: zero new interfaces, one optional constructor parameter, and Microsoft ships the test fake. Want me to apply this to your `InvoiceCalculator`?
